`parseConfiguredEndpoints` enforces "don't mix a Unix socket with network endpoints" (`parsed.size() > 1 && hasSocket`), but `first` never goes through it as it's parsed by `Endpoint.parse` and handed straight to `networkTarget`, which only sees a host string. So the rule applies to `otherEndpoints` and not to the first member.

The test for this passes for a different reason than it looks: `ofEndpointAndUnorderedEndpoints("unix:///var/run/redis.sock", ["node1:6379"])` returns `null` because `DbServerTargetBuilder.sanitizeHost` rejects the slashes, not because of the socket check. A socket path that's also a valid hostname takes the other route:

```java
// renders "redis.sock,node1:6379"
ofEndpointAndUnorderedEndpoints("unix://redis.sock", singletonList("node1:6379"));
// returns null
ofUnorderedEndpoints(asList("unix://redis.sock", "node1:6379"));
```

It doesn't look like this is actually reachable from Redisson, which has no Unix-socket support, but since this is the shared helper for the whole Redis series, running `first` through the same `sharedTarget(first) == null` and `hasSocket` checks might be good to incorporate

